<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The App Sandbox is deliberately OFF, and that is load-bearing rather
	     than an oversight. Two shipped features shell out to system binaries
	     via `Process.run` — `ffmpeg` (video audio and
	     poster extraction) and `open` (SystemMediaOpener). A sandboxed process
	     hands its sandbox down to every child it spawns, so those binaries
	     could not read the recordings directory and `open` could not reach
	     LaunchServices at all. The failures would be the clean, retryable kind
	     the desktop seams are built for, which is exactly what makes them easy
	     to misread: the item just lands `failed` with its source intact, and
	     nothing points at the sandbox.

	     Re-enabling it is a Mac App Store prerequisite, not a security fix to
	     apply casually. It needs, at minimum:
	       com.apple.security.device.audio-input             (record)
	       com.apple.security.network.client                 (HTTP providers)
	       com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only  (file_picker)
	     and even then the three shell-outs stay broken until they have real
	     in-process replacements (Vision for OCR, AVFoundation for video,
	     NSWorkspace for opening a file). Keep this in sync with
	     DebugProfile.entitlements — a sandbox that differs between debug and
	     release means `flutter run` cannot reproduce an installed-app bug. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- Required even though the sandbox above is off, which is the whole
	     surprise. `file_picker` 11 checks this entitlement *itself*, via
	     SecTaskCopyValueForEntitlement on its own task, before it will open any
	     panel — `pickFiles` (every upload capture type) and `getDirectoryPath`
	     (the project editor's repository-path browse button) both fail with
	     PlatformException(ENTITLEMENT_NOT_FOUND) without it, sandbox or no
	     sandbox. Outside the sandbox the key grants nothing and restricts
	     nothing; it is a declaration the plugin reads back off the signature.
	     Read-only is enough: the app only ever reads what the user points at
	     (`saveFile` would need read-write, and is not used). -->
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Hardened runtime enforces library validation: every dylib the process
	     loads must be Apple-signed or carry the app's own Team ID. A local
	     self-signed or ad-hoc identity has no Team ID, so the embedded
	     frameworks (HotKey, FlutterMacOS, plugins) are refused by dyld with
	     "different Team IDs" and the app dies at launch, before Dart runs.
	     Everything in Contents/Frameworks is signed by this same build, so
	     nothing foreign is loaded; the runtime's other protections stay on. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
